# secrets / local config
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
CLAUDE.local.md

# local sessions & generated output
out/
.requivo/
# The demo capture workspace: video, poster frames and a nested session store, none of it referenced
# by src/, scripts/ or docs/. It is the one output root that postdates this block, and it was the
# only untracked entry in `git status` -- one `git add .` away from committing multi-megabyte media
# into a library repo, permanently.
demo-out/

# python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/
venv/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/

# coverage
.coverage
coverage.xml
htmlcov/

# logs
*.log

# packaging
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/

# future web surface (Node)
node_modules/
.next/

# os / tooling
.DS_Store
supertool

# agent tooling: per-session scratch. The 01-oss rule layer itself IS tracked, and
# the reason is not that /oss:scaffold regenerates it -- being regenerated is no
# argument for committing anything. It is that a fresh git worktree takes its rule
# layer from git, and this repo's maintenance loop cuts one worktree per issue, so
# untracking the layer would silently disable it for every delegated agent. It is
# inert for anyone without the plugin that reads it -- CONTRIBUTING.md says so and
# tests/test_agent_layer.py holds it to that (#2). Only this state directory is
# per-session and per-machine.
.claude/jit-context/.discovery/
# The Codex CLI's per-checkout config: which model reviews and at what reasoning effort. That is
# a choice about one developer's account and budget, not about the repository -- the review rubric
# Codex reads, AGENTS.md, IS tracked, because an untracked rubric is absent from every worktree the
# maintenance loop cuts, which is the CLAUDE.local.md trap one tool over.
.codex/
# ...and this, which is per-machine by Claude Code's own convention and is where a personal hook
# would be written. It was never excluded here; it only looked excluded, because this machine
# carries a global ~/.config/git/ignore entry that no contributor has. A hook committed by accident
# would run for everyone who clones -- the barrier #2 reported, made real.
.claude/settings.local.json

# uv lockfile. Requivo is a library: its dependency floors and ceilings are the
# contract, declared in pyproject.toml. A lock here would pin what a consumer
# resolves against and is not what a library ships.
uv.lock
# The agent identity file, wherever it is dropped. It describes the AGENT -- its name,
# voice and values on one machine -- and is per-user by construction, so committing one
# publishes a single developer's setup to everyone who clones. It was tracked at
# .claude/remember/identity.md for a release before anybody noticed.
#
# Both paths are listed on purpose. The remember plugin writes a `.gitignore` holding `*`
# into the store it creates, so a store it made is untracked already; but the store's
# location depends on the mode. In external mode -- `data_dir` in ~/.remember/config.json,
# which is what this machine runs -- the store is OUTSIDE the repository entirely and
# neither of these directories is created by the plugin at all. A file dropped in one by
# hand is then protected by nothing, which is exactly what these two lines are for.
.claude/remember/
.remember/
